# Configuration reload

Every built application exposes a cloneable <Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::reload::ConfigReloader" />. A reload re-reads the manager's retained sources in their original merge order, rebinds changed typed values, runs affected hooks against a complete proposal, and commits only if every stage succeeds.

## Enable the required surface

Manual reload is always available. File watching requires the facade's `watch` feature; SIGHUP is a native Unix trigger.

```toml
[dependencies]
upwell = { version = "1", features = ["watch"] }
```

Automatic setup belongs to <Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::source::ConfigManager" />, not to a standalone watcher:

```rust
use std::time::Duration;
use upwell::prelude::*;

let config = ConfigManager::load_from(&directories, &profiles)?
    .watch_config()
    .reload_on_sighup()
    .config_reload_debounce(Duration::from_millis(500));

let app = App::builder("catalog-api")
    .config_source(config)
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;
```

`App::run` and `App::serve` read those manager-owned triggers after startup succeeds, spawn the tasks, and stop them before shutdown hooks run. Without `watch`, `watch_config()` is logged and ignored at startup rather than making file watching available.

## Reload manually

<Example title="Use the application-owned reloader">

```rust
let reloader = app.config_reloader();
let report = reloader.reload().await?;

tracing::info!(
    generation = report.generation,
    changed = report.changed.len(),
    "configuration accepted"
);
```

The successful report contains the new monotonic generation, changed bindings, and accepting hook reports. An empty `changed` list is a valid successful reload.

</Example>

The same handle is injectable. Use an authenticated administrative route or a deployment control task when the platform has no filesystem notification or signals.

## Validate a proposal with `CfgNext`

<Example title="Reject an unusable pool configuration">

```rust
#[component]
struct DatabasePool;

#[methods]
impl DatabasePool {
    #[hook(ConfigReload)]
    async fn reload(
        &self,
        next: CfgNext<DatabaseConfig>,
    ) -> Result<HookOutcome, PoolError> {
        if next.get().pool_size == 0 {
            return Err(PoolError::EmptyPool);
        }

        self.prepare_pool(next.snapshot()).await?;

        Ok(HookOutcome::Reloaded)
    }
}
```

<Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::CfgNext" /> is read-only and exposes the candidate value, its binding path, and an owned snapshot. The proposal contains staged values for every binding, including unchanged bindings, so a hook with several <code>CfgNext&lt;T&gt;</code> parameters sees one coherent candidate generation.

</Example>

## Understand the transaction

The reload phases are:

1. Re-read every retained source from an empty tree in original precedence order.
2. Seed defaults for every registered binding.
3. Compare each committed binding's raw subtree and consumed placeholder dependencies.
4. Deserialize all changed bindings without modifying live slots.
5. Stage changed and unchanged values into one <Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::reload::ReloadProposal" />.
6. Run affected `ConfigReload` hooks.
7. Commit every prepared swap and adopt the new tree only after all hooks accept.

Load, bind, hook rejection, or guarded deserialization panic produces <Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::reload::ConfigReloadError" /> and leaves every live `Cfg<T>` untouched. A hook that performs external side effects must clean up anything it prepared before returning `Err`; the framework can roll back live config slots, not an arbitrary database connection created by user code.

## Debounce and platform behavior

The default debounce is 250 ms. It coalesces bursts of watcher events; it does not make file replacement atomic. Write deployment config through an atomic rename where possible and rely on transactional rejection for partial or malformed reads.

SIGHUP is Unix-only. File watching is a native filesystem facility. Browser Wasm has neither automatic trigger, but code may still call a server endpoint that performs a manual reload in the native host.

<Callout type="warning" title="A notification is not a commit">

Watcher delivery means a source may have changed. Observe the reload result or generation before assuming live values moved. Rejected candidates preserve the previous generation.

</Callout>

## Troubleshoot reloads

- `Load` means a retained source disappeared, became unreadable, or no longer parses.
- `Bind` means a changed subtree or placeholder cannot deserialize into its registered type.
- `Hook` identifies a proposal rejected by application logic.
- `Panicked` protects the process from a panic during user-provided deserialization; panic payloads are omitted because they may contain secrets.
- No file event usually means `watch` was not compiled, the manager did not request `watch_config`, or the application has not entered `run`/`serve`.
- A changed environment variable can trigger a new binding value on manual reload even when the source subtree is unchanged, but only placeholders consumed by the prior typed read participate in that dependency check.

See <GuideRef slug="framework/configuration/sources-and-bindings">configuration sources</GuideRef> and <GuideRef slug="framework/application/lifecycle">lifecycle and shutdown</GuideRef>.
